Inteligeo, load test of 2026-11-11/12 (no advisory, no upstream fix): the read-only
and maintenance flags are no longer cached in the session, which created a session
for every request that came without one.

Target: GeoNode 4.3.1 as installed by this image (site-packages/geonode). Applied
with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` from site-packages (paths are geonode/...).

What happened: MaintenanceMiddleware and ReadOnlyMiddleware (base/middleware.py)
call configuration_session_cache (base/utils.py) in process_view, which writes
{"config": {read_only, maintenance, expiration}} into request.session. A request
without a session cookie gets a new session, saved to django_session and sent back
as `Set-Cookie: sessionid=...` with SESSION_COOKIE_AGE (14 days). Clients that never
send the cookie back create one session per request:
- GeoServer's REST role service (GeoServerRestRoleService, HttpClient 4), which asks
  /api/users/<name>, /api/roles and /api/adminRole for the roles of every user it
  authorizes. HttpClient rejects the cookie and logs it, value included, as
  `WARN [protocol.ResponseProcessCookies] - Invalid cookie header: "Set-Cookie:
  sessionid=..."` (inteligeo-deploy now silences that logger);
- the liveness and startup probes, migrate_domain.py's wait (/api/v2/), and any
  client with a token or none (QGIS, scripts).
The mirror of production had 351k rows in django_session after the load test. On
production, 695 of these session keys seen in GeoServer's log of 2026-10-05..08 were
all anonymous (user_id null): the session holds only the flags above.
inteligeo-geonode's tokens_vigentes route already set session.modified = False for
the same reason.

The change: configuration_cache() keeps the two flags in the process for 60 s (the
same timeout as before, now per uWSGI process instead of per session) and returns
them; both middlewares read it and no longer touch the session.

What changes in behaviour:
- Turning read-only or maintenance on or off takes effect within 60 s in each
  process, as before it did within 60 s in each session.
- Sessions already holding "config" keep it until they expire; nothing reads it.
- configuration_session_cache no longer exists; nothing else in GeoNode 4.3.1 or in
  inteligeo-geonode calls it.

Not run: GeoNode's tests and a request against a running GeoNode. Checked by reading
and with a stand-in for Configuration (see the commit).

